{"title":"Breaking one factor into a product","key_idea":"You can split one factor into two smaller factors and keep both sides of the multiplication equal.","method":["Look at the factor you need to break apart.","Find two numbers that multiply to make that factor.","Write the multiplication with both smaller factors, then check that both sides are equal."],"worked_example":{"problem":"5 x 10 = 5 x 2 x __","steps":["The factor 10 needs to be split into two smaller numbers.","Find two numbers that multiply to give 10: these are 2 and 5.","Write 5 x 10 = 5 x 2 x 5 and check: 5 x 2 = 10, then 10 x 5 = 50, and 5 x 10 = 50, so both sides match."],"answer":"5"},"key_terms":[{"term":"factor","meaning":"a number you multiply by another number"},{"term":"split or break apart","meaning":"divide a number into smaller parts that multiply together"}],"common_mistakes":["Choosing two numbers that do not multiply to make the original factor; always check that your two new numbers multiply correctly.","Forgetting to check that both sides of the equals sign give the same answer."],"remember":"If you split one factor into two smaller factors, the answer stays the same on both sides.","quick_check":[{"q":"7 x 14 = 7 x 7 x __. What is the missing number?","a":"2, because 7 x 2 = 14"},{"q":"9 x 15 = 9 x 3 x __. What is the missing number?","a":"5, because 3 x 5 = 15"}],"tell_your_child":"Look at the bigger factor and ask yourself what two smaller numbers multiply to make it. Once you find them, one stays with the 6 or 8, and the other is your missing number."}
